S:https://amgueddfa.cymru/casgliadau/caneuongwerin/?id=17 (collected by D. Roy Saer)
N:Sung by T. Morris Owen (born 1887), a Ministry of Defence officer of 15 Goat Street, St David's, Pembrokeshire; recorded 10.9.63 (St Fagans tape 69). A native of Anglesey, he heard it there at the age of 12-14 from farmhands who gathered in the evenings in the village of Llanfachreth, about seven miles from Holyhead. Around the start of the twentieth century the song was especially popular in Anglesey. Malltraeth is on the island's south coast, and its sea embankment, the cob, was finished in 1812. The song keeps up a mock-tragic note until the last verse.
N:Marked on the score for some verses: the quaver D before the dotted A in bar 2 (footnote (a), verses 2 and 3, though "Mall-" is printed under it in verse 1 too), a small B after the C in bar 3 (verse 3), and in bar 11 the G can be two slurred quavers G F (verses 2 and 3). In bar 10 the G can also be split into two quavers G G. The semiquavers B in bar 4 and E in bar 8 are in brackets on the score, for the "(a)" of "(a)rnaf".
